WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
This is the brief on how social media shapes our news.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.000
The apps you're scrolling through every day have completely rewired how we learn about the world,

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
and knowing the hidden science behind your feed is literally your best defense against fake news.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.000
First, social media has officially beaten TV as our main news source.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.000
You know how you stumble onto crazy global events by accident while just swiping for funny videos?

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.000
Well, that's called incidental news.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.000
It's kind of like getting a history lesson from friends at recess instead of a textbook.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.000
It's super relatable, but relying on entertainment apps puts us totally at the mercy of their hidden code.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.000
Second, let's look at how algorithms hook us into doom-scrolling.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Surprisingly, one study showed Twitter's algorithm actually serves slightly more reliable news

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.000
than a normal chronological feed. The real danger is our own brains.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:54.000
We have a negativity bias, meaning our instincts literally make us focus on bad stuff.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.000
Platforms exploit this with the infinite scroll.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:04.000
It's like a chef feeding you spicy junk food just because you keep eating it.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Finally, this endless loop puts our brains on autopilot, leading to the trap of the unread share.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Get this, a massive study found 75% of news links shared on Facebook are posted by people who never even clicked the article.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:20.000
We see a catchy headline, assume we agree, and just hit share.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.000
We would never recommend a movie we haven't watched, so why share news we haven't read?

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.000
So before you hit share on your next doom-scroll, remember that you're the final filter between a catchy headline and the real truth.
